  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1237 PM CDT Tue Aug 25 2026\n\nThis morning, like yesterday morning, there was a 500mb high \nover the southwest United States with several upper level \ndisturbances rotating around this upper high. Southwest Kansas \nremains on the northeast periphery of this upper high, in an \narea of developing northwest flow. One surface boundary today \nwas located from southeast Colorado into the panhandle of Texas.\nFurther north, a cold front was moving towards northwest \nKansas. These two boundaries, along with the moist upslope flow \nthat will be present across southwest Kansas will create a \nfavorable environment for scattered thunderstorms late today and\novernight as our next upper wave exits the Rockies and \napproaches western Kansas. High precipitable water values across\nsouthwest Kansas today (1.4 to 1.8 based on SPC MesoAnalysis) \nis also present which favors the potential for heavy rainfall \nfrom these storms. Based on forecast MUCAPE of +4000j/kg by \nearly evening, high PWAT values and 0-6 shear increasing to 30 \nto 35 knots it currently appears that the main risk from the \nstronger storms storms later tonight and overnight will be \ncapable of producing strong gusty winds, heavy rainfall and \nhail.\n\nTiming of these storms late today and overnight...Our first \nupper wave was currently crossing western Kansas at this time \n(noon), and will move east of the Pratt and Medicine Lodge areas\nby 3pm. As a result, the ongoing convection as of noon today is\nalso expected to end from west to east. These storms will \nproduce gusty winds and heavy rainfall but at this time it \nappears wind gusts may stay in the 50-60 mph. Hail is expected \nto be less than one inch. Once these storms end then our next \nround of thunderstorms are expected to develop between 5pm and \n7pm across eastern Colorado and extreme southwest Kansas (west \nof highway 83) as our next upper level trough begins to cross \neastern Colorado. As these storms move east/southeast across \nwestern Kansas and expand in coverage overnight there will be a \nbetter chance for strong to severe thunderstorms to occur at \ntimes with these storms. At this time the potential exists that \nwind gusts of 60 to 70mph, Quarter size hail and locally heavy \nrainfall. This locally heavy rainfall may result in ponding of \nwater on roads and poor drainage area. \n\nAs our Tuesday night upper wave exit western Kansas, a cold \nfront will cross southwest Kansas and usher in some cooler air \ninto the area. This will bring an end to the hot \ntemperatures...briefly. The ensemble spread of high temperatures\nfollowing this cold front on Wednesday and even Thursday is \nsmall and differs by less than 5f between the cooler 25th \npercentile and warmer 75th percentile. Given this will be \nstaying close to the current NBM high temperature forecast \n(highs in the upper 80s to lower 90s), but temperatures in some \nareas may end up being even a few degrees cooler than this (2-3F\ncooler) on Wednesday depending on how much afternoon cloud \ncover may be present. \n\nThereafter, the upper ridge will reassert its influence on \nwestern Kansas as it shifts east from southwest towards the \nSouthern Plains. This will result in temperatures once again \nrebounding to near 100 degrees by the start of the weekend.",
